<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    Kuna #1628: "x86-64: VMREAD/VMWRITE without REX.W are bad instructions;
    VMX instructions do not set CF/ZF"
    https://github.com/Noelo-Lab/kuna/issues/1628

    clang -O2 -fno-stack-protector of
      unsigned long rd(unsigned long field) {
          unsigned long v; unsigned char fail;
          __asm__ volatile("vmread %2, %0; setna %1" : "=r"(v), "=qm"(fail) : "r"(field) : "cc");
          return fail ? 0 : v;
      }
      int launch(void) {
          unsigned char fail;
          __asm__ volatile("vmlaunch; setna %0" : "=qm"(fail) :: "cc", "memory");
          return fail;
      }
    plus two hand-written functions: wr (vmwrite %rsi,%rdi; setbe %al; movzbl %al,%eax; ret) and
    st (vmptrst (%rdi); ret).

    In 64-bit mode VMREAD and VMWRITE always take 64-bit operands and compilers
    encode them without REX.W (`0f 78 f9`, `0f 79 fe`); the spec only decoded
    the REX.W form, so `rd` was halt_baddata().  VMX instructions report
    VMsucceed / VMfailValid (ZF) / VMfailInvalid (CF) in the flags, and the
    p-code wrote no flags, so the setna after vmlaunch read whatever an earlier
    instruction left in CF/ZF (`&Stack0 <= (char *)0x18`).  VMPTRST stored
    nothing: it passed the loaded qword to its user-op.

    Stage: S1 decode-table / S2 p-code lift (x86 spec-fix, no option).  Each
    VMX user-op returns its status (0 succeed, 1 VMfailValid, 2 VMfailInvalid,
    the __vmx_* intrinsic values) and vmxflags sets ZF/CF from it; VMREAD keeps
    its value output and takes the status from vmx_status.
-->
